;; What a model costs, in USD per 1,000,000 tokens (RFC-025).
;;
;; A RESOURCE, not code, because a price is not a fact about this program: it
;; changes when a vendor changes it, and correcting one should not need a
;; rebuild (RFC-001). A project overrides or extends this with its own
;; `.brainyard/prices.edn`, merged over the top per provider.
;;
;; brainyard keeps the same table as a Clojure literal in
;; `clj-llm/core/usage.clj`, keyed `[provider model]`. Nested here because this
;; is a file people edit, and a nested map diffs and reads better than a
;; hundred two-element vector keys.
;;
;; `:cache-read` and `:cache-write` are ABSOLUTE rates, not multipliers of
;; `:input`. A model with no prompt caching simply omits them — and note that
;; an absent rate costs exactly what a zero would, since the token counts are
;; zero too.
;;
;; **A price is curation.** Nothing here is generated or refreshed
;; automatically, which is brainyard's policy and worth keeping: a wrong price
;; that arrived by itself is harder to notice than a missing one, because a
;; missing one reports as UNKNOWN (RFC-025) rather than as a number.
;;
;; Only the providers this port can actually reach are priced. `claude-code`
;; is absent on purpose — the CLI reports its own `total_cost_usd`, which is
;; what it really billed, and a table cannot beat that. `openrouter` and
;; `freellm` resell at their own margins, so the underlying model's rate would
;; be a guess; `ollama` is local and `mock` is free. `codex` (Codex CLI on a
;; ChatGPT plan, byj-gly.430.9) is priced from `:openai`, as the API would bill
;; its model (`cost/rates-for`): its CLI reports tokens, not a cost.

{:anthropic
